  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1226 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nTonight through Tuesday will see a battle of low pressure across \nthe Rockies and a stubborn, slow to exit high pressure over the \nmidwest and Great Lakes area. What this means for the local area is \na tightening surface pressure gradient through Tuesday and above \nnormal temperatures. Given the stronger winds aloft and the \ntightening gradient, southerly winds will likely gusts to 25 to 35 \nmph on Sunday and Monday and possibly as high as 45 to 50 mph on \nTuesday. The increased winds are in response to the low pressure \nover the Rockies shifting across the Western High Plains.\n\nWith precipitation not expected during this time, grasses will \ncontinue to cure after a wet September/October as well as soils \nlikely losing some saturation as well. This suggests that we may see \nsome elevated fire weather concerns Sunday, Monday and especially \nTuesday. The good news is the majority of the harvest is over so \nfire starts will be less likely. \n\nAs the trough to the west deepens and moves eastward, southerly flow \nincrease Tuesday, increasing warm advection across the region. \nWhile some stratocumulus will be possible, instability is pretty \nmuch non-existent and the dgz remains dry, so precipitation is not \nexpected, or will remain very isolated.\n\nStrong southerly flow remains in place into Wednesday as a stronger \npiece of energy ejects into the Northern and Central Plains. We will \nsee better saturation within the dgz as well as the potential for \nsome very weak instability, so some very late afternoon and evening \nshowers will be possible. This will likely be a short period of lift \nas a strong dry punch follows this mid and upper level wave, \nshutting down precipitation chances for Thursday.\n\nOnce this wave passes the next, stronger wave will move into the \narea Friday into Saturday. While agreement on the finer, and \nimportant details is somewhat low, most models and model runs are \nbringing this stronger energy into the area. The latest models and \nensembles as well as AI output are all hinting at a good chance for \na half an inch of rain or more to the area Friday into Saturday, \nwith the better chance Friday night. Given the steep mid level lapse \nrates some elevated convection will also be possible which could \nfocus these higher rainfall amounts into a smaller area. As for \nprecipitation type, for now with a weak northern stream upper level \njet, warmer low level air should surge north and remain in place \nhelping to keep the main precipitation type rain. As colder air \nspills in behind a brief changeover to snow would be possible but \nfor now this does not look like it will bring any moderate or major \nwinter weather impacts to the area.",
